## Preflight — before activity filtering or any marker write

1. Resolve this checkout's physical root (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, then `cd`
   and `pwd -P`; outside Git use `pwd -P`). Identity is verified by `authorize`
   below; a directory name or the mere presence of a skill is insufficient.
2. For automatic recording, run
   `${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/skills/setup-memory-workflow/scripts/check-drift.sh authorize __PROJECT__ auto`.
   This checks the current installed skill identity, resolved project and consent bound
   to this physical checkout. Missing, revoked, invalid, unsupported or mismatched
   `.basic-memory-workflow.json` denies automatic recording. Stop before notes AND
   `.claude/.last-saved-session`; offer a one-time save or scoped enrollment.
   Only if the user directly requested this save, use `authorize __PROJECT__ once`
   instead. That explicit request is sufficient; do not ask again or create standing
   consent. “I'm done” alone is an offer trigger, not permission. Never use `once`
   merely to get past a denied automatic check.

All note and search calls below pass `project="__PROJECT__"` explicitly. Reads need no
recording consent. Recovery of another transcript needs an explicit request naming that
transcript and covering recovery/save; a standing grant is insufficient. Read and curate
only that transcript, never mining other transcripts or broad session-index content, and
never advance `.claude/.last-saved-session` from it — recovered work must not mark the
current conversation as covered. Maintenance is separate and manual.

## Step 0 — Meaningful activity

After authorization, automatic saves require more than a trivial lookup or a couple of
exchanges AND a persistable decision, resolved item, finding or convention. File edits
are not required. If nothing qualifies, report a no-op and record an evaluated checkpoint
using “On success” below. Direct save requests skip the activity pre-filter, but do not
invent content.

## BM 1.x write contract — applies to every step

Use `output_format="json"` for reads whose checksums are consumed and all writes.
Immediately before each mutable-note edit, obtain a fresh `bm_read` of that identifier
and pass its checksum as `expected_checksum` to `bm_edit(command=...)`. Use the returned
path/identifier for subsequent operations. `bm_edit` never creates missing notes.
Create only confirmed-missing notes with `bm_write(..., overwrite=false)`; valid forms
include `identifier` or `title` plus `directory` and `note_type`.

For a targeted edit, the replacement argument is named `content` — never guess a
different name for it:

```
bm_edit(command="find_replace", identifier=<path>, find_text=<exact existing text>, content=<replacement text>, expected_checksum=<fresh checksum>, project="__PROJECT__", output_format="json")
```

`bm_read` paginates via `start_line`, `end_line` and `max_tokens` only — it has no
`page` argument.

Verify each structured receipt against the installed contract: a documented successful
`action` for the requested operation, the intended returned `file_path`/identifier and a
nonempty checksum. A missing `project` field is fine; an explicitly conflicting project
or qualified path is a failure. Treat `isError`, typed refusals, missing/malformed
receipts and uncertain outcomes as failures. On `REVISION_CONFLICT`, stop and report it
(see On failure). A success-looking text message alone is insufficient.

## Step 1 — Read status and resolve the active log

Read `status/__PROJECT__-current-status.md` with `bm_read`. On confirmed `NOT_FOUND`,
prepare a new status note at that canonical path; other read failures stop the save.
A size/token-limit failure needs explicit maintenance, not improvised trimming.

From the most recent dated Status Summary entry, resolve the **last wikilink** to the
active session log. Use its returned identifier. If no link exists, use scoped
`bm_search(query="__PROJECT__ session", project="__PROJECT__")`
and inspect credible session-log candidates. More than one plausible candidate requires
a user choice; do not guess. Search errors are not an empty result. If successful scoped
discovery finds no log, create `sessions/__PROJECT__-session-log.md` with `bm_write`,
`overwrite=false`, a session-log title and initial banner. Retain the receipt's exact
identifier for the status link and Step 2; do not derive it from the title. If that path
already exists, stop and resolve it instead of overwriting it.

Before drafting the new Status Summary entry, run
`${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/skills/setup-memory-workflow/scripts/check-drift.sh status-bloat`. A nonzero
exit means the note already meets or exceeds its oversized thresholds (≥8,000 characters,
≥200 lines, or any single line ≥800 characters): write only a short pointer line — noting
that the save occurred and that `/save-session-maintenance` is needed — instead of a full
entry, and add no further narrative content until maintenance has run. On exit 0, write a
normal capped entry as described below.

Update the status using targeted `bm_edit(command="find_replace" or "replace_section")`
with a fresh checksum, or create the confirmed-missing canonical status via `bm_write`.
Preserve the existing Open list as the baseline; move only named items actually resolved
this session, add new open work and changed environment facts, update “Last updated”.
Keep bd/OpenSpec as task authority and spot-check still-active work. Do not regenerate
Open from this session alone, resize or restructure the whole note.

This is forward-only: items already in the Open section as freeform bullets are left
exactly as they are; only a *new* Open item created this session uses the pointer shape
below. For a new item: on confirmed-missing `open-items/` directory or schema note,
create the `type: schema`/`entity: open-item` note (consult `bm_man(page="bm-schema(3)")`
for exact Picoschema authoring syntax if uncertain — required fields `status` (accepts
`open`/`stale`/`resolved`), `found_session`, `last_touched_session`, optional
`severity`), then create
`open-items/<slug>.md` with `bm_write(overwrite=false)` carrying that frontmatter and the
item's `[category]` observation line as its body, then add one line to the status note's
Open section: `- [<category>] <short title> — [[Open: <slug>]]`. Frontmatter shape:

```
---
type: open-item
status: open
found_session: 2026-11-17
last_touched_session: 2026-11-17
---
```

**Touch tracking**: if this session's work references, updates, or reaffirms an
*existing* pointer-ized Open item (one with an `[[Open: <slug>]]` line already in the
status note), fresh-read that item's note and update its `last_touched_session` field to
today's dated entry via checksum-guarded `bm_edit`; this is the only time an existing
`open-items/` note's frontmatter changes outside of resolution or maintenance.

**Resolving a pointer-ized item**: when a named item with an `[[Open: <slug>]]` line is
actually resolved this session, update its note's `status` to `resolved` (mirroring how a
plain prose Open item already moves to Resolved) and remove its line from wherever it
currently appears — the live Open section if it has its own pointer line, or the Stale
Open Items Index note if it was already rolled up there (same removal operation,
different target note).

Write the new dated Status Summary entry as explicit short lines, not one flowing
paragraph, capped at 5 physical lines: a date line, 1-3 short content lines or bullets,
and a `Detail: [[...]]` line pointing at the exact active log resolved above. Anything
that doesn't fit belongs in the session log, not a longer entry. Example:

```
**2026-11-17**
- Fixed the `bm_edit`/`bm_read` tool-call examples and added the status-bloat gate.
- Collapsed the SessionStart hook chain to one library-sourcing pass.
Detail: [[Session Log — chezmoi]]
```

Verify the receipt before continuing.

## Step 2 — Append to the resolved session log

Curate only from the current conversation and available current-session context — mining
other transcripts needs separate recovery authorization (see Preflight). Include today's
date, decisions/findings/changes and items resolved. Open items and next steps belong in
status, not this log entry.

Freshly `bm_read` the resolved log and use `bm_edit(command="append", content=...,
expected_checksum=<fresh checksum>, project="__PROJECT__", output_format="json")`.
Never overwrite, roll over or triage it during a routine save. Verify the receipt.

## On success — authorized evaluation only

Only after both writes have confirmed receipts (or a legitimate authorized no-content
Step 0 evaluation), update the current session's checkpoint. Re-run the same `authorize`
mode used in Preflight 2 immediately before the marker write; if it fails, leave the
marker intact. From the verified project root, locate exactly one current-session
transcript:

```bash
[ -n "${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID:-}" ] || exit 1
TRANSCRIPT=$(find "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/projects" -name "$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID.jsonl")
[ -f "$TRANSCRIPT" ] || exit 1
TURN_COUNT=$(jq -s '[.[] | select(.type=="user" and (.toolUseResult // null)==null and (.isMeta // false)==false)] | length' "$TRANSCRIPT") || exit 1
printf '%s:%s\n' "$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID" "$TURN_COUNT" > .claude/.last-saved-session
```

If the transcript cannot be identified/counting fails, report that the notes were saved
but the checkpoint was not advanced. Confirm both saved note titles and returned
permalinks/paths, or explicitly report no-op.

## On failure

Stop and identify the failed step/error and every already-confirmed write. The two notes
are not a transaction: if status succeeds but append fails or is uncertain, report a
partial save and leave the marker unchanged. Do not roll back unrelated edits, claim
coverage, or blindly replay a possibly successful append. Inspect its outcome before any
separately authorized retry.
